# GitFlow (SDLC)

Use classic GitFlow. Do not invent alternate branch naming (e.g. `cursor/…`) for feature work.

## Long-lived branches

| Branch | Role |
|--------|------|
| `main` | Production-ready only. Fast-forward or merge from `release/*` / `hotfix/*`. Tag releases. |
| `develop` | Integration branch. Default base for day-to-day work and PRs. |

Never commit feature work directly to `main` or `develop`.

## Supporting branches

| Type | From | Merge to | Name |
|------|------|----------|------|
| Feature | `develop` | `develop` | `feature/<short-kebab-name>` |
| Release | `develop` | `main` **and** `develop` | `release/x.y.z` |
| Hotfix | `main` | `main` **and** `develop` | `hotfix/x.y.z` or `hotfix/<short-kebab-name>` |

## Agent / contributor workflow

1. `git checkout develop && git pull`
2. `git checkout -b feature/<name>` (or `release/…` / `hotfix/…`)
3. Commit on the supporting branch only
4. Open a PR **into `develop`** for features; release/hotfix PRs follow the merge targets above
5. After merge, delete the local and remote supporting branch
6. Do not reopen a merged feature branch for unrelated work—start a new `feature/*` from updated `develop`

## PRs

- Feature PRs target `develop`
- Keep the branch focused; one concern per feature branch when practical
- Do not force-push `main` or `develop` unless the user explicitly requests it
